    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` rule: when more than 80 owners have already been created today (registered on this owner's adjusted business-day registration date), the created owner carries `bulkSignupWarning: true`; otherwise `false`. It's captured at creation time and surfaced on `GET /api/owners/{id}`, mirroring how `namesakeCount` works.\n\nChanges:\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 set `owner.setBulkSignupWarning(countOwnersRegisteredOn(registrationDate) > 80)` before saving, reusing the same daily-count path as the existing per-day sign-up limit rule.\n- **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a persisted `bulk_signup_warning` column with getter/setter (Boolean). MapStruct auto-maps it onto `OwnerDto` by matching name, so no mapper change was needed.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n- **DB schemas** \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column to hsqldb (used by tests), h2, mysql, and postgres schema files.\n\nThe threshold semantics: the daily-limit rule already rejects at \u2265100 owners, so the warning is effectively true for the 81st\u201399th owner of the day and false otherwise \u2014 the fresh-create case the test asserts returns `false`.",
